const e={"seo.purpose":"Who the passport name tool helps","seo.purpose.p1":"Anyone applying for a first Taiwan passport who has to decide how their name is spelled, or family members filling in the form for a child or parent.","seo.purpose.p2":"It also helps before English CVs, study-abroad or visa forms, when you want to see your Chinese name in all four romanizations.","seo.why":"Why use our passport name tool","seo.why.p1":"Every syllable comes from the Bureau of Consular Affairs passport romanization table (updated 2026-05-29). No spelling is made up.","seo.why.li1":"Hanyu Pinyin, Tongyong, Wade-Giles and Gwoyeu Romatzyh II side by side, written the passport way: WANG, XIAO-MING.","seo.why.li2":"Pick the reading of characters that have several; type the Zhuyin yourself for characters the dictionary lacks instead of letting it guess.","seo.why.li3":"Compound surnames, a spouse's surname and the hyphen in given names follow the examples in the bureau's Q&A.","seo.why.p2":"Your name is matched in your browser and never uploaded. If you already hold a passport, keep its spelling; this is for reference only.","seo.faq":"Passport name FAQ","seo.faq.q1":"Do I have to use Hanyu Pinyin?","seo.faq.q1a":"No. The bureau lists all four systems as a reference and respects the applicant's choice, though your surname should match your family's.","seo.faq.q2":"Can I switch to a new spelling if I already have a passport?","seo.faq.q2a":"Generally no. Renewals and replacements keep the old Latin name; changes are allowed only in cases such as a mismatch with the national-language reading (once) or a surname that differs from close relatives.","seo.faq.q3":"Is the hyphen in the given name required?","seo.faq.q3a":"No. It just marks syllables: CHEN, LI-LING may also be CHEN, LI LING or CHEN, LILING, and the tool can switch between them.","seo.faq.q4":"Why are ㄅ and ㄆ both P in Wade-Giles?","seo.faq.q4a":'The passport table writes Wade-Giles without apostrophes, so some letters repeat. A few syllables such as ㄍㄜ are listed as "KE or KO", and the tool shows both.'};export{e as default};
